me":{"start":1788196946856,"end":1788196946861}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"A `books.db` file was created by the module-level `app = create_app()` call at import time (when tests imported `app`). That's a side effect. I should guard that so importing the module in tests doesn't create a file. Let me make the module-level `app` only created when run as `__main__`, OR better, only create it lazily. Actually the cleanest is to not create the module-level app at all, and instead use a `flask` convention. But the dev server (`python app.py`) needs `app` at module level for some runners. Let me guard it so it doesn't run when imported under tests.\n\nSimplest: keep `app = create_app()` but it creates books.db on import. To avoid that side-effect polluting the workspace, I can make the module-level app creation lazy, or skip it when not __main__. Let me just remove the module-level `app` and put it inside `__main__`. But some tooling (e.g. `flask run`) expects an `app` module attribute. Compromise: create it lazily via a function, or guard with an env check.\n\nActually, for `flask run` to work, the app must be at module level. But the side effect of creating books.db on import is acceptable for production. The issue is only that tests pollute the workspace. I already deleted books.db. Let me make the module-level creation conditional: only create if not being imported by pytest. A clean way: check `if __name__ != \"__main__\"` skip... no, that breaks `flask run`.\n\nBetter: don't auto-create books.db at import. Use a lazy proxy. Actually simplest: remove module-level `app`, and in `__main__` create it. For `flask run`, document `--app app:create_app`. Let me do that — it's cleaner and avoids the import side effect.\n\nLet me also delete the stray books.db.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_058d80399001ohej7XjrTFDq41","sessionID":"ses_fa72b1d29ffe24CcENoHmfOP3D","messageID":"msg_058d7d859001klY8pfWXUolAVq"}}
