{"type":"text","timestamp":1788196969293,"sessionID":"ses_fa72b1d29ffe24CcENoHmfOP3D","part":{"id":"prt_058d84ada001XI44Ikvu3ap5Rn","messageID":"msg_058d843a0001nuoBbe4QdVdbSf","sessionID":"ses_fa72b1d29ffe24CcENoHmfOP3D","type":"text","text":"Done. Implemented a Flask + SQLite book collection REST API per `TASK.md`.\n\n**Deliverables:**\n- `app.py` — Flask app with `POST/GET/GET{id}/PUT/DELETE` on `/books`, `?author=` filter, `GET /health`, SQLite storage, JSON responses with correct status codes (200/201/204/400/404), and validation (title & author required, year must be int).\n- `test_app.py` — 8 tests (well over the required 3) covering health, validation, full CRUD lifecycle, missing-book 404s, empty list, and type validation. Each uses an isolated in-memory DB.\n- `requirements.txt` — `flask>=3.0`.\n- `README.md` — setup, endpoints, config (`BOOKS_DB_PATH`, `PORT`), run instructions, and curl examples.\n\n**Verification:** `pytest -v` → 8 passed. A smoke test against the file-based DB also confirmed `create`/`list`/`health` work outside the test harness.\n\nKey implementation note: in-memory SQLite uses a unique shared-cache URI per app instance (`app.py:38`) so tests are isolated while still letting separate per-request connections see the same schema.","time":{"start":1788196965082,"end":1788196969291}}}
